          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Goneril's love for Lear as a true emotional state. The rest of her turn intensifies the claimed degree of that love, so truth-presenting avowal is its clear dominant mode, regardless of whether the claim is sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The occurrence does not create, renew, or invoke a commitment by Goneril. Her immediate elaboration measures and praises the extent of a daughter's love but states no duty, allegiance, future conduct, or acceptance of an obligation. Lear's announced and subsequently conferred bounty is Lear's act in response to the competitive avowal, not an undertaking made by Goneril through “I love you.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"That we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"Although the language is intense and claims that love exceeds speech, the expression is a composed answer to Lear's formal question rather than a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The expression answers Lear's contest-like question and may serve a strategic or flattering purpose, but its core force remains the comparative avowal captured by T. The supplied evidence does not establish a separate residual speech-act mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public dynastic proceeding, Lear asks his daughters to say which loves him most and explicitly links their answers to the distribution of his bounty. Goneril, called on first, addresses Lear with an elaborate comparative declaration of filial love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No undertaking by Goneril is established. She avows exceptionally great filial love but does not bind herself to duties or future conduct. Lear treats her answer as sufficient grounds for granting her territory, but that is uptake of her answer in the love test, not acceptance of a promise from her. The scene alone does not settle her sincerity.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare's King Lear. That recognition is not used to add facts beyond the supplied passage.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied evidence establishes the avowal's public, competitive setting but does not independently settle whether Goneril actually feels the love she professes or intends any unstated obligation.\"}"
        }
